### 💡 Real-Life Analogy
A HashMap is like a row of 16 mailboxes. You compute a hash code from the name on the envelope to determine which mailbox number to open, finding the letter inside in O(1) time!

### ⚡ Core Rule & Syntax
```java
// HashMap Internals
Map<String, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();
map.put("Kishore", 98);

// Key Contract Rule:
// If two objects are equal according to equals(), 
// they MUST produce the exact same hashCode()!
```

### 🎯 Placement Interview Tip
When asked 'What happens if you override equals() without overriding hashCode()?', answer: **'The HashMap contract is broken. Two logically equal keys will generate different hashCodes, landing in different buckets, so map.get(key) returns null!'**

### 💡 Real-Life Analogy
A checked exception is like a passport check before an international flight: the airline won't let you board unless you show it (compiler enforces handling).
An unchecked exception is tripping on a stair: an unexpected runtime stumble caused by a programming error.

### ⚡ Core Rule & Syntax
```java
// Try-With-Resources automatically closes AutoCloseable streams
try (BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new FileReader("data.txt"))) {
    String line = br.readLine();
} catch (IOException e) {
    System.err.println("Read error: " + e.getMessage());
}
```

### 🎯 Placement Interview Tip
When asked 'What is the difference between `volatile` and `synchronized`?', reply: **'`volatile` guarantees visibility of variable updates across CPU caches without blocking, but does NOT guarantee atomicity. `synchronized` guarantees both visibility and mutual exclusion (atomicity) by locking.'**

### 💡 Real-Life Analogy
An Activity lifecycle is like an actor's performance day: waking up backstage (`onCreate`), walking onto the stage (`onStart`), looking directly at the cheering audience ready to act (`onResume`), taking a brief pause when phone rings (`onPause`), stepping offstage (`onStop`), and going to sleep (`onDestroy`).

### ⚡ Core Rule & Syntax
```kotlin
class MainActivity : ComponentActivity() {
    override fun onCreate(savedInstanceState: Bundle?) {
        super.onCreate(savedInstanceState)
        // Initialize dependencies and UI bindings
    }
    override fun onResume() {
        super.onResume()
        // Resume camera, location listeners, audio
    }
    override fun onPause() {
        super.onPause()
        // Pause sensors and release heavy resources
    }
}
```

### 🎯 Placement Interview Tip
When asked 'What happens when a user rotates their Android phone?', answer: **'The OS destroys the current Activity (`onPause -> onStop -> onDestroy`) and instantiates a brand new Activity (`onCreate -> onStart -> onResume`) with new configuration resources.'** ViewModels retain state across this cycle without restarting queries.

### 💡 Real-Life Analogy
Imperative UI (old XML) is like giving a painter step-by-step orders: *"repaint button border green, now if clicked, repaint it red"*.
Declarative Compose is describing the finished painting: *"render button with color based on `isClicked` state"*. When state changes, Compose automatically redraws the affected parts!

### ⚡ Core Rule & Syntax
```kotlin
@Composable
fun CounterScreen() {
    // remember survives recomposition; rememberSaveable survives rotation!
    var count by rememberSaveable { mutableIntStateOf(0) }

    Column(modifier = Modifier.padding(16.dp)) {
        Text(text = "Count: $count")
        Button(onClick = { count++ }) {
            Text("Increment")
        }
    }
}
```

### 🎯 Placement Interview Tip
When asked 'How do you optimize Recomposition in Jetpack Compose?', mention 3 techniques: **State Hoisting** (pass only values and lambdas), **using `@Stable` or `@Immutable` data classes** so Compose knows objects cannot change without new instances, and **using `derivedStateOf`** for high-frequency state like scroll offsets.

### 💡 Real-Life Analogy
Coroutines are lightweight virtual workers. Instead of hiring 1,000 expensive heavy trucks (heavy OS threads), you hire 1 truck with 1,000 nimble couriers that pause (`suspend`) without blocking the driver and resume when their delivery arrives!

### ⚡ Core Rule & Syntax
```kotlin
class HomeViewModel(private val repo: CourseRepository) : ViewModel() {
    // StateFlow holds immutable UI state
    private val _uiState = MutableStateFlow<UiState>(UiState.Loading)
    val uiState: StateFlow<UiState> = _uiState.asStateFlow()

    fun fetchCourses() {
        viewModelScope.launch(Dispatchers.IO) {
            val result = repo.getCourses()
            _uiState.update { UiState.Success(result) }
        }
    }
}
```

### 🎯 Placement Interview Tip
When asked 'What is Structured Concurrency in Kotlin?', answer: **'It guarantees that child coroutines cannot outlive their parent CoroutineScope. If a parent scope is cancelled or an unhandled exception occurs, all running child jobs are automatically cancelled, preventing memory and thread leaks!'**

### 💡 Real-Life Analogy
Dependency Injection is like having your computer custom-built by an expert assembly shop according to your parts list (`@Inject`), instead of you fabricating silicon chips, soldering motherboards, and wiring power cables yourself in your bedroom!

### ⚡ Core Rule & Syntax
```kotlin
// Room DAO with Reactive Flow
@Dao
interface SubjectDao {
    @Query("SELECT * FROM subjects ORDER BY title ASC")
    fun getAllSubjects(): Flow<List<SubjectEntity>>

    @Insert(onConflict = OnConflictStrategy.REPLACE)
    suspend fun insertAll(subjects: List<SubjectEntity>)
}

// Retrofit API Interface
interface SynapseApi {
    @GET("curriculum/subjects")
    suspend fun getSubjects(): Response<List<SubjectDto>>
}
```

### 🎯 Placement Interview Tip
When asked 'How does Room notify UI when the database table updates?', reply: **'When a DAO method returns a `Flow<T>`, Room observes table invalidation triggers in SQLite. Whenever any INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE touches that table, Room automatically queries the new dataset and re-emits down the Flow!'**
